   "content": "<p>Surah al-An‘am, verse number 152</p><p>وَلَا تَقْرَبُوْا مَالَ الْيَتِيْمِ اِلَّا بِالَّتِيْ هِىَ اَحْسَنُ</p><p>Translation: And do not even go near the property of the orphan except in a way that is very good.</p><p>I.e. the orphan whose guardianship becomes the responsibility of some Muslim, it is his duty to wish him well in every way. The demand of this goodwill is that if from his property, i.e. from inheritance, whatever share has come to him, whether it is in the form of cash or in the form of land and estate, and he is not yet capable of protecting it, then his property should be protected with full sincerity until he reaches the age of puberty and discernment. It should not be that, in the name of guardianship, his property or estate is disposed of before his age of discernment.</p><p>وَاَوْفُوا الْكَيْلَ وَالْمِيْزَانَ بِالْقِسْطِ</p><p>Translation: And give full measure and weight with justice.</p><p>I.e. making shortage in measuring and weighing — taking full measure and weight while receiving, but not doing so while giving, rather giving others less by tipping the scale. This is an extremely low thing, fallen from morals. In the nation of Shu‘aib (peace be upon him) this very moral disease existed, which became the cause of their destruction.</p><p>لَا نُكَلِّفُ نَفْسًا اِلَّا وُسْعَهَا</p><p>Translation: We do not burden anyone except according to his capacity.</p><p>I.e. the things which We are emphasising are not such that acting on them is difficult. If it were so, We would not have given their command, because We do not make anyone responsible beyond his capacity.</p><p>وَاِذَا قُلْتُمْ فَاعْدِلُوْا وَلَوْ كَانَ ذَا قُرْبٰى</p><p>Translation: And when you say something (about someone), say it with justice, even if he is your relative.</p><p>I.e. when you decide cases, decide with justice and fairness.</p>",
