   "content": "<p>8. Avoiding israf, i.e. not adopting extravagance (tabdhir).</p><p>9. Eating good and pure things and avoiding impure things.</p><p>10. Above all, considering Allah Most High alone the real Sustainer and knowing one’s efforts and wisdom to be His gift.</p><p>11. Distributing out of the property granted by Him, in His way, among deprived classes.</p><p>From the above-mentioned points it is proved that the economic system of Islam provides the guarantee of economic stability. By paying zakat and charities the lust and greed for property and wealth ends from the heart of man. In this way his purification of the self takes place. The saying of the Exalted is:</p><p>خُذْ مِنْ اَمْوَالِهِمْ صَدَقَةً تُطَهِّرُهُمْ وَتُزَكِّيْهِمْ بِهَا (التوبہ: 103)</p><p>Translation: O Prophet, take charity from their property and purify them and purify their selves.</p><p>When the self of wealthy people is purified, then they do not take part in such economic activities as become the cause of economic instability or from which harm reaches the interest of the common people.</p><p>In brief we can say that by spending in the way of Allah the economic system gets stability. Economic unevenness becomes less and the problems of deprived classes become somewhat less, from which they become a cause of increase in national productive capacities.</p><p>Responsibilities of the individual and society</p><p>With reference to spending in the way of Allah some responsibilities fall on the individual and society, without fulfilling which social and economic stability cannot be established.</p><p>1. The first responsibility that falls on every individual is that he spend all his energies for earning a livelihood. He should leave no stone unturned in bringing his abilities into use.</p><p>2. The second responsibility is that for earning wealth he should keep in view the basic teachings of Islam. Islam has certainly given encouragement for earning a livelihood, but it has also settled basic rules in business life. The wealth earned by means of interest, or the profit obtained by adulteration, or property obtained from prohibited sources in the same way — Islam has declared it forbidden. Property earned in this way, even if all of it is spent in the way of Allah, there is no benefit of it in the hereafter.</p>",
