   "content": "<p>v. Equality</p><p>By giving the principle of economic, social and human equality, Islam has made all human classes stand in a single row. In the Islamic state the rich and the poor, and the ruler and the subjects, are equal in the eye of the law. The Noble Prophet ﷺ had made it clear that no Arab has superiority over a non-Arab, nor a white over a black. The criterion of superiority is taqwa. All individuals of society are equal in the eye of the law. No particular class will have autonomy over the resources of the country. Economically all people will get equal opportunities, and as human beings the honour, dignity, life and property of all people are sacred.</p><p>vi. Taqwa</p><p>The foundation of the first Islamic society was laid on taqwa. The purpose of all acts of worship was declared to be the attainment of taqwa. This is the principle which is the touchstone of honour, respect and nobility in society. It is the emotion of the fear of God that a person holds within himself. In fact taqwa is that emotion which creates the distinction between good and evil in the heart. Taqwa is the quality that inclines a person towards good deeds and compels him to run away from evil. A God-fearing person fears doing every such deed which may cause the displeasure of Allah the Exalted, and does with a happy heart such deeds from which the pleasure of Allah the Exalted is obtained.</p><p>vii. Self-sacrifice and sympathy</p><p>Among the foundations of the first society, one important foundation was also the attainment of the spirit of self-sacrifice and sympathy. Since a Muslim is the brother of a Muslim, he is extremely sympathetic and caring towards his brother. One of the most important characteristics of Islamic society is that spirit of self-sacrifice and sympathy which keeps urging a Muslim to give preference to the needs of others over his own needs. A Muslim does not abandon his brother in a difficult time. In the Islamic society whose foundation the Noble Prophet ﷺ laid, this spirit was present to the fullest degree.</p><p>viii. Rights of the people</p><p>The edifice of the first Muslim society had been established on such an imperishable system of rights and duties whose parallel no …</p>",
