   "content": "<p>Verse no. 281</p><p>وَاتَّقُوْا يَوْمًا تُرْجَعُوْنَ فِيْهِ اِلَى اللّٰهِ ۗ ثُمَّ تُوَفّٰى كُلُّ نَفْسٍ مَّا كَسَبَتْ وَهُمْ لَا يُظْلَمُوْنَ</p><p>Translation: And fear that day when you will return to the presence of Allah, and every person will receive the full recompense of his deeds, and no loss will be done to anyone.</p><p>Explanation</p><p>The meaning of this blessed verse can easily be understood by reading it together with the previous verse. In the preceding verse, giving respite to debtors was mentioned, and besides that forgiving the loan was declared the best deed. In this verse it has been said that in this regard fear Allah and keep this in mind, that one day you have to appear before God.</p><p>If a person&#x27;s faith is firm and he thinks at every moment that one day he has to die — whatever wealth is with him is to remain in this very world — then he will not hesitate at all in giving respite to the debtor, and forgiving the loan too will not be difficult for him. We should always keep this matter in view, that whatever is with us is all the favour of Allah the Exalted. Everything is in the grip of His power. Whomever He wishes He grants abundance of provision, and whomever He wishes He gives hardship. The will and pleasure of Allah the Exalted is precisely this, that through the disparity of wealth and riches He tests His servants and sees who spends more out of his wealth and who adopts stinginess and miserliness. We should, if someone seeks to take a loan from us, certainly help him and then give him ease in the return of the loan. Allah the Exalted will give us the reward for it.</p>",
