   "content": "<p>Kinds of khabar with respect to chains (isnad)</p><p>• Hadith marfu&#x27;: this is that hadith in which a saying, deed, tacit approval or attribute of the Noble Prophet ﷺ is mentioned, and some Companion states it.</p><p>• Hadith mawquf: this is that hadith in which the saying, deed or tacit approval of a Companion is mentioned.</p><p>• Hadith maqtu&#x27;: this is that hadith in which the saying, deed or tacit approval of a Tabi&#x27;i is mentioned.</p><p>Kinds of khabar with respect to narrators</p><p>☆ Khabar wahid, with respect to the number of narrators, is of three kinds.</p><p>1) Khabar mashhur: this is that hadith whose narrators are in no age fewer than three, but it does not reach the level of mutawatir.</p><p>2) Khabar &#x27;aziz: this is that hadith whose narrators are in no age fewer than two.</p><p>3) Khabar gharib: this is that hadith in whose sanad, somewhere or other, only one narrator remains.</p><p>Kinds of khabar with respect to the qualities of narrators</p><p>☆ Khabar wahid, with respect to the qualities of its narrators, is of 12 kinds.</p><p>1) Sahih li-dhatihi: this is that hadith all of whose narrators are of perfect precision, the sanad is connected, and no hidden defect or irregularity is found in it.</p><p>2) Hasan li-dhatihi: this is that hadith in which all the qualities of sahih li-dhatihi are present, but some deficiency is found in memory and precision.</p><p>3) Da&#x27;if (weak): this is that hadith among whose narrators the conditions of sahih li-dhatihi and hasan li-dhatihi are not present.</p><p>4) Sahih li-ghayrihi: this is that hadith which was originally hasan li-dhatihi, but its several chains were found, so it became sahih li-ghayrihi.</p><p>5) Hasan li-ghayrihi: this is that weak hadith which has many chains.</p><p>6) Mawdu&#x27; (fabricated): that hadith is called so, in which a lie in hadith is proved against one of its narrators.</p><p>7) Matruk (abandoned): that hadith whose narrator is accused of lying, or whose narration is against the known facts of religion and established rules.</p><p>8) Shadhdh (irregular): this is that narration whose narrator is himself trustworthy, but he is contradicting more trustworthy narrators than himself.</p><p>9) Mahfuz (preserved): that narration which is opposite to the shadhdh.</p><p>10) Munkar (denounced): that hadith whose narrator, despite being weak, is contradicting the trustworthy.</p>",
