   "content": "<p>… those narrators through whom the hadith reaches us are called the sanad.</p><p>When at the end of the Banu Umayyah and the beginning of the caliphate of the Banu Abbas the ahadith were collected, then this science began. In a branch of this science, &quot;Asma&#x27; al-Rijal&quot;, the life-circumstances of more than five hundred thousand people were collected, and through it this became known: which narrator is reliable, and which ahadith are sahih, which hasan, which da&#x27;if and which rejected.</p><p>v. The science of fiqh</p><p>The verbal meaning of fiqh is &quot;to know something&quot;, to make clear and open the real meaning of something. In Shari&#x27;ah terminology, the knowledge of the issues of Shari&#x27;ah deeds is called &quot;fiqh&quot;. Among these issues, issues of every kind of worship and dealings are included. The science of fiqh has four basic sources: the Quran, the Sunnah, qiyas (analogy) and ijma&#x27; (consensus). The regular beginning of the science of fiqh started in the era of Hazrat Amir Mu&#x27;awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) in 41 Hijri. In this era Hadith was being compiled (mudawwan), therefore a distinction was made between hadith and opinion, and the regular science of fiqh began. As a result of it four great schools of fiqh were formed. The first of them is Imam Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him). His school attained the greatest fame. It is called the Hanafi school. The second is Imam Shafi&#x27;i (may Allah have mercy on him), whose school is called Shafi&#x27;i; the third is Imam Malik (may Allah have mercy on him), who was in the Hijaz; they are called Maliki; and the fourth is Imam Ahmad bin Hanbal (may Allah have mercy on him), whose school is called Hanbali. All these noble jurists made the laws of the Islamic Shari&#x27;ah clear in book form. All are authentic and reliable and exactly in accordance with the Shari&#x27;ah. In every era people of various regions are their followers. The largest number of people are followers of Hanafi fiqh.</p><p>vi. Principles of fiqh (Usul al-Fiqh)</p><p>The science of usul al-fiqh discusses the proofs of Shari&#x27;ah issues. In this science too, great progress took place in the Abbasid era, and countless books were written, and with great success this science was compiled. It provides guidance for those who came later. An authoritative book on usul al-fiqh is &quot;al-Risalah fi &#x27;Ilm al-Usul&quot; of Imam Shafi&#x27;i (may Allah have mercy on him). Besides this, the books of Imam Muhammad (may Allah have mercy on him) and Imam Abu Yusuf (may Allah have mercy on him) etc. are worth mentioning.</p><p>vii. Tasawwuf (Sufism)</p><p>In different eras some change has been found in the meaning and sense of tasawwuf. But on this matter all agree that tasawwuf is in fact the name of the purity of the heart and the purification of the soul. The real Sufi is only he who at all times [keeps] his bond with Allah, the Lord of Honour …</p>",
