   "content": "<p>Among the other names worth mentioning in chemistry are Muhammad Ibn Zakariya Razi, Abu al-Qasim Majriti and al-Kathi. Translations of their books have been made into Latin and other Western languages. Besides them, Ibn al-Wahshiyyah, Ya&#x27;qub bin Ishaq al-Kindi and Ibn Sina etc. too are included among Muslim chemists.</p><p>Physics</p><p>Physics is that science which in English is called (Physics). In it the study of mechanics, heat, light, magnetism, planets and space travel is included. Besides translating Greek books, Muslims invented many new concepts in this branch from which the whole world is benefiting till today. For example, the theories of Ibn al-Haytham, which are mostly about light and the eye. Among his famous works is &quot;al-Manazir&quot;, in which complete information on &quot;light&quot; exists. Besides this he has many other books and works too. Bu Ali Sina studied natural bodies (matter), their motion and rest, and discovering many new things played his important role in physics. In the book &quot;al-Mu&#x27;tabar&quot; of Abu al-Barakat al-Baghdadi, discussion on the topic of physics exists.</p><p>The Chinese and Greeks were acquainted with magnetic attraction, but Muslim scientists for the first time made its practical benefits evident in the form of the compass, through which the art of navigation progressed. It was first mentioned in the Jawami&#x27; of Muhammad al-&#x27;Awfi. The relation of the eye and light and its properties, the invention of the camera, methods of weighing precious stones, the principles of the specific weight (Specific Gravity) of other liquids, methods of finding the degree of temperature, and basic concepts like the formation of the rainbow — these are the achievements and services of Muslim scientists alone.</p><p>Astronomy</p><p>It is also called &#x27;Ilm al-Hay&#x27;ah and (Astronomy). In astronomy Muslims accomplished prominent achievements. In this science the geometric structure of the universe is discussed, under which the distance of celestial bodies and the rules and regulations of their motions are laid down. For explaining these motions moving models (models) are made, which [are called] tables …</p>",
