   "content": "<p>c. Educational progress</p><p>Although free and compulsory education up to matric is part of our constitution, practically till now it could not be fully implemented. Only 59 per cent Pakistanis are literate. Among girls the rate of literacy is even lower. On the occasion of the first educational conference in Pakistan, which was held in Karachi on 27 November 1947, the Quaid-e-Azam (may Allah have mercy on him), through his message, had laid emphasis on acquiring scientific and technical education, so that in the coming days our economic condition may be stable and the self-respect of our people may not be wounded. The Quaid-e-Azam (may Allah have mercy on him) had also laid emphasis on bringing change in the educational system for building the morals and character of the young generation. Even today, if our educational system is reviewed, then the lack of two elements is felt intensely.</p><p>a. Instead of general education, professional, technical and scientific education should be made common.</p><p>b. Through education positive attitudes should be created in the morals and character of youth.</p><p>The Government of Pakistan has recently prepared a comprehensive programme for making technical education common at the provincial and federal level. Through the Higher Education Commission, free higher education inside and outside the country is being provided to the capable young generation. It is hoped that soon its effects will begin to come before the country and nation, and educationally Pakistan will become a modern Islamic welfare state.</p><p>d. Establishment of social equality</p><p>One purpose of the establishment of Pakistan was also this, that all people obtain social and legal equality. Although constitutionally and legally no one in Pakistan has exemption, yet practically we often observe that unfortunately some influential people are considered more important and more worthy of respect socially. The law-making provincial and national assemblies of Pakistan are serious about such law-making according to which all will get equal social importance, and Pakistan will in reality emerge as an Islamic welfare realm.</p><p>On one occasion the Quaid-e-Azam (may Allah have mercy on him) had said:</p><p>&quot;The constitution of Pakistan is yet to be made. The Constituent Assembly of Pakistan will make it. I do not know what the actual form of …</p>",
