   "content": "<p>[al-Hamd] To praise with reverence, or to give thanks.</p><p>[lillah] At the beginning of the word Allah, &quot;lam&quot; is a preposition. About the word &quot;Allah&quot; the opinion of the scholars is that by inserting alif-lam on the word &quot;ilah&quot;, &quot;Allah&quot; was formed, and this word is specific to the essence of Allah the Exalted.</p><p>[Rabb] This is derived from tarbiyah and is synonymous with muslih (reformer). The origin is that bringing something gradually to perfection is called tarbiyah. The meaning of tarbiyah is to set right the affairs of another. Moreover Rabb has many meanings, such as Owner, Reformer, the One worshipped etc.</p><p>[al-&#x27;Alamin] This is a derived noun; its singular does not occur. It includes all creatures besides Allah.</p><p>[al-Rahman al-Rahim] Both these are derived from the word &quot;al-Rahmah&quot;. &quot;al-Rahman&quot; is an intensive form on the pattern of fa&#x27;lan. The quality found in an intensive form is not permanent, like ghadban (being in anger). &quot;al-Rahim&quot; is an adjective on the pattern of fa&#x27;il. The quality found on the pattern of fa&#x27;il is permanent, like &#x27;alim (one of very much knowledge). The scholars have written that the attribute of al-Rahman is for all creatures, and the attribute of al-Rahim is specific to Muslims.</p><p>[al-Din] Its meaning is recompense, reward.</p><p>[iyyaka na&#x27;budu wa iyyaka nasta&#x27;in] The meaning of &#x27;ibadah is the expression of utmost humility and utmost lowliness, whereas the meaning of isti&#x27;anah is seeking help.</p><p>[al-Sirat] Its meaning is path.</p><p>[an&#x27;amta &#x27;alayhim] The people upon whom Allah the Exalted bestowed favour; they are the prophets, the truthful, the martyrs and the righteous.</p><p>[al-maghdubi &#x27;alayhim] Those people upon whom the wrath of Allah the Exalted fell. Its referents are the Jews and their followers.</p><p>[al-dallin] By the misguided are meant the Christians and those who walk in their footsteps.</p>",
