   "content": "<p>Commentary:</p><p>This surah is supplicatory. At the beginning is praise and glorification, then the expression of the servant&#x27;s humility, and then supplication.</p><p>In literary terms there is the style of iltifat (turning) in it, that is, at the beginning there is the third-person form, then changing from the third person it becomes the second-person form.</p><p>This surah is a model of eloquence and rhetoric.</p><p>&quot;Iyyaka na&#x27;budu&quot; originally should have been &quot;iyyahu na&#x27;budu&quot;, but in it there is iltifat from the third person towards the second person. And because of the object coming before the subject, specification has been made: that O Allah, we do not worship anyone except You. And the same construction is of &quot;iyyaka nasta&#x27;in&quot; too, and (means) we ask help specifically from You alone.</p><p>Like all the surahs of the Holy Quran, this surah too is a high model of Arabic literature.</p><p>Surah al-Fajr</p><p>Introduction:</p><p>This is a Makki surah. In this surah three important topics are mentioned.</p><p>1. Mention of some former nations who belied the messengers of Allah, and then the punishment of Allah descended upon them, such as the people of &#x27;Ad, the people of Thamud and the people of Pharaoh.</p><p>2. Mention of the practice of Allah the Exalted of testing His servants by means of good and evil, and by means of wealth and poverty; moreover mention of man&#x27;s intense love for wealth.</p><p>3. Mention of the abode of the Hereafter and its hardships; moreover on the Day of Judgement the division of human beings into the fortunate and the unfortunate, and the end of good and evil souls.</p>",
