   "content": "<p>Commentary:</p><p>Hazrat Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) is proverbial in justice. He (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote this letter to one of his judges, Hazrat Abu Musa al-Ash&#x27;ari (may Allah be pleased with him), to make him aware of the responsibilities of his office. In this letter too we will find unfamiliar words seldom; rather such words have been used which fulfil their purpose. The style of exception (istithna&#x27;) has been used in the best manner, as in &quot;wa al-sulhu ja&#x27;izun bayna al-muslimin illa sulhan&quot;; there is balance in the sentences.</p><p>The letter of the Commander of the Faithful Hazrat Uthman bin Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) to his officials</p><p>اَمَّا بَعْدُ! فَاِنَّ اللّٰهَ اَمَرَ الْاَئِمَّةَ اَنْ يَّكُوْنُوْا رُعَاةً وَّلَمْ يَتَقَدَّمْ اِلَيْهِمْ اَنْ يَّكُوْنُوْا جُبَاةً وَّاِنَّ صَدْرَ هٰذِهِ الْاُمَّةِ خُلِقُوْا رُعَاةً، وَلَمْ يُخْلَقُوْا جُبَاةً، وَلَيُوْشِكَنَّ اَئِمَّتُكُمْ اَنْ يَّصِيْرُوْا جُبَاةً وَّلَا يَكُوْنُوْا رُعَاةً، فَاِذَا عَادُوْا كَذٰلِكَ انْقَطَعَ الْحَيَآءُ وَالْاَمَانَةُ وَالْوَفَآءُ، اَلَا وَاِنَّ اَعْدَلَ السِّيْرَةِ اَنْ تَنْظُرُوْا فِيْ اُمُوْرِ الْمُسْلِمِيْنَ وَفِيْمَا عَلَيْهِمْ. فَتُعْطُوْهُمْ مَا لَهُمْ وَتَاْخُذُوْهُمْ بِمَا عَلَيْهِمْ ثُمَّ تُثَنُّوْا بِالذِّمَّةِ فَتُعْطُوْهُمُ الَّذِيْ لَهُمْ وَتَاْخُذُوْهُمْ بِالَّذِيْ عَلَيْهِمْ ثُمَّ الْعَدُوَّ الَّذِيْ تَنْتَابُوْنَ فَاسْتَفْتِحُوْا عَلَيْهِمْ بِالْوَفَآءِ (الطبری، تاریخ الامم والملوک (4/244,245))</p><p>Translation: To proceed! So Allah has given the imams (that is, the amirs and chiefs) this command, that they be guardians; and before them it was not like this, that they be collectors of wealth. And the early people of this Ummah were made guardians; they were not made collectors of wealth. And it is near that your (amirs and chiefs) will certainly become collectors of wealth and will not remain guardians. So when they become like this, then modesty, honesty and loyalty will end in them. And indeed the most just conduct is this, that you look after the affairs of the Muslims and …</p>",
