   "content": "<p>… is even faster than the blink of an eye, and because of this reflection the sciences and arts are born, which we mentioned earlier. Then man, because of this thought — and not only man but the animal too — is inclined towards acquiring his innate and natural desires. So the faculty of thought inclines towards acquiring the sciences not present with it. And he either turns towards those of more knowledge and gnosis than himself, or those who acquired knowledge from the former prophets, and attribute the propagation of acquired knowledge to them; and he is eager for acquiring it and for knowledge.</p><p>Lexical explanation</p><p>[al-sakan] Home, dwelling, place of refuge.</p><p>[al-muhayyi&#x27;] Ready.</p><p>[ikhtilaj al-fikr] The agitation of thought.</p><p>[asra&#x27;] Faster.</p><p>[lamh al-basar] The time of the blink of an eye.</p><p>[tastad&#x27;i] To want, to desire.</p><p>[al-idrakat] Sciences.</p><p>[talaqqahu] Acquired it.</p><p>Commentary</p><p>Allama Ibn Khaldun has gathered many sciences in his Muqaddimah. The extract under view has been taken from the sixth chapter of the Muqaddimah. Acquiring knowledge and giving teaching are part of human nature. In it he has told that man is distinguished from other animals because of intellectual reasoning, and he always uses this faculty of thought: how to acquire a livelihood? How to acquire knowledge? For acquiring the knowledge of things of which he has no knowledge, he turns to those of more knowledge than himself. Allama Ibn Khaldun is the founder of &#x27;umraniyyat (sociology). He was the first to lay down its principles. In his style there is fluency and flow.</p><p>&#x27;Awarif al-Ma&#x27;arif</p><p>This book has been written by Shaykh Shihab al-Din Suhrawardi (may Allah have mercy on him). It is a book written on the topic of tasawwuf. His name is Shaykh Shihab al-Din Abu Hafs Umar bin Muhammad al-Suhrawardi. He (may Allah have mercy on him) was born in Suhraward in 529 Hijri. He kept the company of Shaykh Abd al-Qadir Jilani (may Allah have mercy on him).</p>",
