   "content": "<p>Translation: And in this year 187 Hijri Hazrat Fudayl bin &#x27;Iyad (may Allah have mercy on him) passed away. His (may Allah have mercy on him) kunyah was Abu Ali, and his birthplace was in Khurasan. He came to Kufah and heard (hadith) from Mansur Ibn Mu&#x27;tamir and others. Then he became devoted to worship, went to Makkah, and stayed there till his death.</p><p>Hazrat Sufyan bin &#x27;Uyaynah (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated and said: The Caliph Harun al-Rashid invited us, so we entered his court, and Fudayl entered last of all. He had covered his head with his sheet. So he said to me: O Sufyan, which of them is the Commander of the Faithful? I said: this is, and I pointed towards Harun al-Rashid. Then he said to him: O you of handsome face! You in whose hand and on whose neck is the rein of this Ummah — you have taken the responsibility of a very great work. So the Caliph Harun al-Rashid wept. Then he gave each person a purse of ten thousand dirhams. So everyone except Fudayl accepted it. Then Harun al-Rashid said: O Abu Ali, if you do not consider it lawful for yourself, then give it to some debtor, or feed some hungry person to his fill, or clothe some naked person. So he excused himself from taking it. Then when we came out, I said: O Abu Ali, you made a mistake; if you had taken it and spent it in works of good (it would have been better). So he took hold of my beard, then said: O Abu Muhammad, you are the jurist of the city and people look towards you, and you make such a mistake? If this was lawful for those people, then it would be lawful for me too.</p>",
